---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { site } from "../site";
---

<Legal
  title="Acceptable Use Policy"
  description="What you can and can't send with send0, how we enforce it, and how to report abuse."
  summary="send0 is for conversations people start or expect: sign-ups, replies, support, operations. It is not for cold outreach, bulk mail or marketing. We enforce this automatically and by hand, on every account."
>
  <h2 id="scope">1. Who this applies to</h2>
  <p>
    This policy applies to everyone who uses the send0 hosted service ("send0 Cloud"), including the AI agents and
    software acting on your behalf. You are responsible for everything sent or received through your account, whether
    a person or an agent did it. It forms part of our <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you self-host the open-source edition, this policy doesn't govern your instance, but your email provider's own
    rules and the law still do.
  </p>

  <h2 id="allowed">2. What send0 is for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Signing up for services and receiving verification codes, magic links and password resets</li>
    <li>Replying to people who wrote to your agent, and continuing those conversations</li>
    <li>Support, sales-operations, procurement, scheduling and collections conversations with people who expect to hear from you</li>
    <li>Transactional and operational mail that a recipient requested or reasonably expects</li>
    <li>Testing your own sign-up, password-reset and notification flows</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="prohibited">3. What you may not do</h2>
  <h3>Unsolicited and bulk mail</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Send cold outreach: mail to people who haven't contacted you, agreed to hear from you, or have no existing relationship with you</li>
    <li>Send newsletters, marketing campaigns, promotions or other bulk mail</li>
    <li>Use bought, rented, scraped or harvested address lists</li>
    <li>Rotate inboxes, domains or accounts to get around sending limits or suppression</li>
    <li>Keep mailing an address after it has bounced, complained or asked you to stop</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Deception and fraud</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Phishing, or collecting credentials, payment details or personal data under false pretences</li>
    <li>Impersonating a person, company, government body or brand, including through display names or look-alike addresses</li>
    <li>Fraud, scams, advance-fee schemes or fake invoices</li>
    <li>Forging or hiding message headers or the true origin of a message</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Harmful content</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Malware, ransomware or links to them</li>
    <li>Harassment, threats, stalking or hateful content targeting people</li>
    <li>Content that sexualises minors, or any illegal content</li>
    <li>Content that infringes someone else's intellectual property or privacy</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Abusing the platform</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Creating accounts to evade a suspension, or creating large numbers of accounts automatically</li>
    <li>Using send0 inboxes to create fake accounts at scale on other services, or to break another service's terms</li>
    <li>Probing, scanning or attacking send0 or anyone else's systems, or interfering with other customers</li>
    <li>Reselling send0 or giving others access to your account without our written agreement</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">4. Sending limits and controls</h2>
  <p>To protect deliverability for everyone, these controls apply automatically:</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Reply-only free tier</td><td>Free accounts can send only to addresses that have already emailed that inbox, plus the account owner's verified address.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Verified accounts</td><td>Every account must verify its email address. Disposable and temporary email addresses can't sign up.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gradual limits</td><td>New accounts can send up to 50 messages a day. Limits rise as an account builds a clean sending record.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Automatic pause</td><td>Sending pauses if an account's complaint rate passes 0.3% or its hard-bounce rate passes 5%.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Suppression</td><td>Addresses that hard-bounce or complain are suppressed automatically and can't be mailed again from your account.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Custom domains for volume</td><td>Sending beyond the starter limits requires a verified custom domain with SPF, DKIM and DMARC.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reserved addresses</td><td>postmaster, abuse, admin, support, noreply, security and billing can't be claimed as send0.email inboxes.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every message must identify its sender honestly. Ongoing conversational mail must stop when a recipient asks, and
    any message that isn't a direct reply must give the recipient an easy way to opt out.
  </p>

  <h2 id="enforcement">5. Enforcement</h2>
  <p>
    We monitor bounce rates, complaint rates, blocklist reports and abuse reports. Depending on how serious a problem
    is, we may warn you, lower your limits, pause sending, suspend an inbox or close your account. For clear abuse
    such as phishing or malware we act immediately and without notice. We may also report illegal activity to the
    relevant authorities.
  </p>
  <p>
    If we pause or suspend something, you'll receive an <code>inbox.suspended</code> event and an email explaining
    why. You can reply to that email to appeal, and a person will review it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">6. Reporting abuse</h2>
  <p>
    If you received unwanted mail from a send0 address, or think someone is misusing send0, email
    <a href={`mailto:${site.email.abuse}`}>{site.email.abuse}</a>. Include the full message with headers if you can.
    A person reads every report, and we aim to respond within one business day. Security vulnerabilities go to
    <a href={`mailto:${site.email.security}`}>{site.email.security}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">7. Changes</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy as the service and the email ecosystem change. We'll post the new version here and,
    for significant changes, email account owners before they take effect.
  </p>
</Legal>
